WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.720
Building a modern web application almost
universally assumes a certain

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.640
level of heavy machinery.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.280
Developers rely on a massive dependency
tree, complex tooling, and

4
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.400
a build pipeline to compile code before it
ever goes

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.720
live. Consider the WolfClan martial arts
operations platform.

6
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.800
It features a marketing site, operations
hub, and member portal,

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.360
connecting to a database and stripe
billing.

8
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.920
Yet, this system relies on exactly zero
frameworks, zero NPM

9
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.240
packages, and zero build steps.

10
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.600
There is no React.

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.600
There is no Next.js.

12
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.680
Strip away the expected machinery, and the
raw skeleton underneath

13
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.240
is purely pure HTML, CSS, and vanilla
JavaScript.

14
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.120
It raises a quiet architectural question.

15
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.280
How does a highly functional, highly
secure platform operate when

16
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.560
we entirely abandon the black box
machinery we've been taught

17
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.280
is mandatory? The deployment philosophy
relies on edge-first hosting, where

18
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.840
simple servers scattered around the world
handle requests.

19
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.480
The mental model is incredibly direct.

20
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.120
Files go up, the site comes down.

21
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.720
By skipping the build cache completely, no
deployment pipelines to

22
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.240
debug, no sprawling dependency tree to
audit, and no framework

23
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.240
release cycles, the entire application
relies on a single file

24
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.520
acting as middleware, a central
interceptor for traffic.

25
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.040
It totals exactly 480 lines of code.

26
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.600
This file acts as the master traffic cop
for the

27
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.280
entire platform. It reads the URL path and
decides exactly

28
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.520
what data the user is allowed to see.

29
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.440
The public site bypasses authentication
entirely, while the member portal

30
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.640
checks a session token against the
database before injecting member

31
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.240
data into the request.

32
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.760
Removing the abstraction of a sprawling
middleware chain forces the

33
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.160
security boundary out into the open.

34
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.680
The entire access logic sits in one highly
readable, instantly

35
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.720
auditable location. Without a frameworks
abstraction layer, you write the

36
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.400
security code yourself.

37
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.520
You also have to read that security code
yourself.

38
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.920
This diagram shows a subtle vulnerability
in standard authentication.

39
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.360
The top half visualizes a naive password
check.

40
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.400
If a user types the wrong first letter,
the server

41
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.600
immediately stops checking and returns an
error faster than if

42
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.600
they got the first five letters right.

43
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.600
Attackers can measure those microscopic
time differences to guess passwords

44
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.720
letter by letter.

45
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.400
To fix this, the custom middleware uses a
timing-safe comparison

46
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.280
on SHA256 password hashes.

47
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.640
Every single comparison takes the exact
same amount of time,

48
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.320
completely closing the leak.

49
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.240
For state-changing actions, the server
runs a strict origin validation

50
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.280
check. It verifies that the request is
actually coming from

51
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.120
the expected domain, without relying on
external security libraries.

52
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.920
The member portal abandons passwords
altogether.

53
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.920
Entering an email generates a single-use
token link with a

54
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.840
15-minute lifespan. That link gets emailed
out, authenticating the user

55
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.320
upon click. Over in the operations hub,
markdown documents are

56
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.120
served using a hard-coded allow list.

57
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:06.760
Instead of using clever sanitization to
prevent directory traversal attacks,

58
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.200
where users trick the server into showing
hidden files, the

59
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.360
system simply checks an array.

60
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.560
If your requested file isn't on the
approved list, you

61
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.320
get a 404 error.

62
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.680
These are highly robust vanilla
implementations.

63
00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:25.560
They prove that digital security doesn't
require rigid third-party opinions.

64
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.920
It requires well-understood, transparent
patterns.

65
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.680
Standard engagement tracking counts page
views or clicks.

66
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.560
That tells the club someone loaded a page,
but it

67
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.600
fails to capture actual continuous
presence.

68
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.240
This graph illustrates a custom solution.

69
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.520
Built entirely without a framework, the
member portal sends a

70
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.240
lightweight heartbeat signal back to the
server every 30 seconds

71
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.760
during an active session.

72
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.840
Logging those continuous heartbeats
generates incredibly precise data.

73
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.080
The club can calculate average session
duration and track consistency,

74
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.280
allowing them to identify members who are
slowly drifting away

75
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.520
and check in on them before they cancel.

76
00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.120
But operating without a framework
introduces rigid costs.

77
00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.280
Without a component templating system,
user interface elements like headers

78
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.240
and footers have to be manually copied and
pasted across

79
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.160
multiple HTML files.

80
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.880
The system also sacrifices type safety.

81
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.560
Because it uses vanilla JavaScript instead
of TypeScript, which catches

82
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.520
errors before code goes live, a simple
typo in a

83
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.480
database column name will only surface as
a runtime error

84
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.600
for the end user.

85
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.440
And managing raw CSS files without
dedicated tooling means there

86
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.880
is no automated dead code elimination and
no minification to

87
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.400
compress file sizes.

88
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.560
The costs of this raw transparency are
genuine, but for

89
00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.320
a project at this specific scale,
absorbing those minor inefficiencies

90
00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.680
is vastly cheaper than maintaining the
heavy, complex build tooling

91
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.040
required to prevent them.

92
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:54.280
Deconstructing an application down to its
vanilla roots is not

93
00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.960
a universal mandate to abandon modern
frameworks.

94
00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.480
This approach thrives under highly strict
conditions.

95
00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.120
It works best for small teams of one or
two

96
00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.960
developers operating in edge-first hosting
environments.

97
00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.720
Crucially, it requires a security model
that is straightforward enough

98
00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.240
to audit by reading the raw code yourself,
rather than

99
00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.440
trusting the maintainers of a third-party
dependency.

100
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.120
It is an architecture reserved for
environments where raw deployment

101
00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.640
speed, absolute control, and transparency
take absolute priority over developer

102
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:31.720
experience. Modern frameworks are
brilliant tools, but they are not

103
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.760
mandatory prerequisites for building on
the web.

104
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.240
The most sophisticated architecture isn't
the one packing the most

105
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.520
hidden dependencies, it's the one where
you can confidently hold

106
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:41.920
the

